# A beam of sun in the air, which is the one thing participating media exists to draw.
#
# Everything else in this renderer can be judged on a lit surface. A medium cannot: with no
# medium the sun through an opening is a bright patch on the floor and nothing in between, and
# with one it is a shaft you can see the shape of. So the scene is the smallest thing that shows
# the difference -- a sealed dark room with one small opening in its roof, and no other light in
# it at all, so that anything visible in the air got there by scattering.
#
# Sealed on purpose. A room with a doorway would be lit by whatever came through the doorway, and
# the beam would have to be picked out of that. Here the only light in the picture is the sun
# through the oculus, the patch it lands on, and what that patch bounces back.
metre 32
bounds -4 -0.6 -4   4 4.4 4

# The walls are nearly black on purpose, and it is the point of the scene rather than a mood.
# A grey room bounces the sun patch back into itself, and everything in the air then has two
# possible causes -- the beam, or a wall glowing at it. Take the bounce away and there is only
# one thing left that can put light in the middle of the room.
material stonefloor rgb=198,192,182 rough=220
material stonewall  rgb=26,25,24    rough=235
material stonerim   rgb=120,117,112 rough=205

# The shell, and the room hollowed out of it. Walls are 0.4 m thick, which at this scale is
# thirteen voxels -- enough that no light leaks through them and the oculus is the only way in.
let bm_shell  = box -3.4 -0.4 -3.4   3.4 3.9 3.4
let bm_room   = box -3.0  0.0 -3.0   3.0 3.5 3.0

# The oculus. Round, and 0.84 m across: wide enough that the shaft is a shaft rather than a
# thread, narrow enough that the room around it stays dark and the beam has an edge to look at.
# The sun in this renderer stands up and to one side, so the shaft leans and lands off centre --
# which is worth more than a vertical one, because a leaning beam shows its own length.
let bm_oculus = cylinder 0 3.7 0 r=0.42 h=1.4 axis=y

let bm_all = difference { bm_shell bm_room }
let bm_all = difference { bm_all bm_oculus }

# The floor, so the patch the beam lands on is a pale surface against dark walls and the two ends
# of the shaft -- where it comes in and where it arrives -- are both in the frame.
let bm_slab = box -3.4 -0.4 -3.4   3.4 0.01 3.4

# A rim around the opening, so the edge the shaft is cut by is visible as geometry rather than
# having to be inferred from the shaft.
let bm_rim = cylinder 0 3.7 0 r=0.62 h=1.4 axis=y

paint stonewall
paint stonerim   where=bm_rim  below=0.03
paint stonefloor where=bm_slab below=0.02

variation colour=0.018 rough=0.030 seed=3

solid bm_all
